        ":question/problem": "Use the substitution $u=3 x$. Which integral is obtained?\n\n$$\n\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}} \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=3 x$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=3.\n$$\n\nTransform both bounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}&\\Longrightarrow u=\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\\\x=\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}&\\Longrightarrow u=2 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}} \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\int_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi} \\sin{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformed integral is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\int_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi} \\sin{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
