        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=2 x$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nTransform both bounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}&\\Longrightarrow u=\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\\\x=\\pi&\\Longrightarrow u=2 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}}^{\\pi} 3 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\frac{3}{2}\\left(\\int_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi} \\sin{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformed integral is\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}\\left(\\int_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi} \\sin{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\n$$",
