    {
      "knowledge_point": {
        "db_id": 17592186068915,
        "id": "746d1bc6-98ec-59a1-bec6-9dcfb7081a5d",
        "title": "Integrating Trigonometric Functions With Linear Arguments"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "e448a4d2-65fb-5da5-83af-e67ab30b203d",
        ":question/problem": "Evaluate exactly.\n\n$$\n\\int_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{8}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} 3 \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nYou may use $\\int \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\sin{\\left(u \\right)}+C$.\n\nEndpoint values: $\\sin{\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{2} \\right)}=-1,\\quad \\sin{\\left(2 \\pi \\right)}=0$.\n\n{{answer}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=4 x$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=4.\n$$\n\nTransform both bounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{3 \\pi}{8}&\\Longrightarrow u=\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\\\x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}&\\Longrightarrow u=2 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{8}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} 3 \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\frac{3}{4}\\left(\\int_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi} \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\left[\\frac{3 \\sin{\\left(u \\right)}}{4}\\right]_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi}=\\left(0\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{3}{4}\\right).\n$$\n\nThe answer is\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
          {
            ":answer-field/id": "866f3cb3-d08c-55fc-9741-2055767f7e3a",
            ":answer-field/key": "answer",
            ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
