      "question": {
        ":question/id": "6ea83d61-8ef7-5554-9089-4e7d6658d120",
        ":question/problem": "Find an antiderivative.\n\n$$\n\\int \\cos{\\left(3 x + 2 \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nYou may use $\\int \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\sin{\\left(u \\right)}+C$.\n\n{{answer}} $+C$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=3 x + 2$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=3.\n$$\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int \\cos{\\left(3 x + 2 \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\int \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate with respect to $u$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\int \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right)=\\frac{\\sin{\\left(u \\right)}}{3}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute back:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sin{\\left(3 x + 2 \\right)}}{3}+C.\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
