    {
      "knowledge_point": {
        "db_id": 17592186068915,
        "id": "746d1bc6-98ec-59a1-bec6-9dcfb7081a5d",
        "title": "Integrating Trigonometric Functions With Linear Arguments"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "ee47bd77-b3fc-5c96-b3c1-1120ca3a9273",
        ":question/problem": "Evaluate exactly.\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}} \\sin{\\left(\\pi - 4 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nYou may use $\\int \\sin{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=- \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}+C$.\n\nEndpoint values: $\\cos{\\left(\\pi \\right)}=-1,\\quad \\cos{\\left(0 \\right)}=1$.\n\n{{answer}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=\\pi - 4 x$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-4.\n$$\n\nTransform both bounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\Longrightarrow u=\\pi\\\\x=\\frac{\\pi}{4}&\\Longrightarrow u=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new bounds decrease. Keep this order when evaluating the antiderivative.\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}} \\sin{\\left(\\pi - 4 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=- \\frac{1}{4}\\left(\\int_{\\pi}^{0} \\sin{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\left[\\frac{\\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{4}\\right]_{\\pi}^{0}=\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{1}{4}\\right).\n$$\n\nThe answer is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
          {
            ":answer-field/id": "7f4984fe-084f-51a5-bb0d-e7dc322c9849",
