      "question": {
        ":question/id": "e7ec123b-4f1a-58be-a8aa-4b6e169ef8a2",
        ":question/problem": "Find an antiderivative.\n\n$$\n\\int 2 \\cos{\\left(1 - 2 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nYou may use $\\int \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\sin{\\left(u \\right)}+C$.\n\n{{answer}} $+C$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=1 - 2 x$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-2.\n$$\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int 2 \\cos{\\left(1 - 2 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=-\\left(\\int \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate with respect to $u$:\n\n$$\n-\\left(\\int \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right)=- \\sin{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute back:\n\n$$\n- \\sin{\\left(1 - 2 x \\right)}+C.\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
