      "question": {
        ":question/id": "9891ad60-b02c-519b-bb3b-3ccdc5f192ce",
        ":question/problem": "Evaluate exactly.\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} \\cos{\\left(\\pi - 3 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nYou may use $\\int \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\sin{\\left(u \\right)}+C$.\n\nEndpoint values: $\\sin{\\left(\\pi \\right)}=0,\\quad \\sin{\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\right)}=1$.\n\n{{answer}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=\\pi - 3 x$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-3.\n$$\n\nTransform both bounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\Longrightarrow u=\\pi\\\\x=\\frac{\\pi}{6}&\\Longrightarrow u=\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new bounds decrease. Keep this order when evaluating the antiderivative.\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} \\cos{\\left(\\pi - 3 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=- \\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\left[- \\frac{\\sin{\\left(u \\right)}}{3}\\right]_{\\pi}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}=\\left(- \\frac{1}{3}\\right)-\\left(0\\right).\n$$\n\nThe answer is\n\n$$\n- \\frac{1}{3}.\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
